Question 5 (Combined)
Indonesia is entering a digital-first era, with Gen Z and Millennials becoming a major force in the economy and consumer market. Based on 2025 demographic data, Indonesia has approximately 284.7 million people, with Gen Z and Millennials representing 49.27% of the total population — nearly half of the country. These digital-savvy generations increasingly expect banking to be personalized, transparent, relevant, and instantly rewarding. They are accustomed to personalized promotions, loyalty rewards, gamification, and time-sensitive offers across digital platforms such as marketplaces, hotels, and ticketing — where dynamic pricing is already the norm. For example, the same product can be sold at different prices across platforms, such as flight tickets priced differently between tiket.com and Traveloka.
Yet, dynamic pricing remains largely untapped in banking, particularly for funding products like TAKA Online and Deposito Online. Today, OCBC applies uniform pricing to all customers within a product tier, without accounting for real-time variations in market conditions, the bank's liquidity needs, or individual customer profiles — despite customers having different transaction histories, product holdings, balances, profitability, and engagement levels. This creates a clear gap: the bank treats different customers similarly, while customers expect increasingly personalized value. A standardized pricing approach limits the bank's ability to optimize both customer conversion and margin, while giving customers little incentive to deepen their relationship or stay loyal.
For the Emerging Affluent segment, predominantly tech-savvy Gen Z and Millennials, this creates a significant opportunity to reinvent pricing: moving from "one rate for everyone" to "the right rate for the right customer at the right time." Funding products are a natural starting point for this shift, as they tend to be easier to manage — their tenors are longer and liquidity is more predictable — allowing the bank to offer rates that are more optimal from a treasury perspective while remaining attractive to specific customers, without sacrificing product simplicity.
